  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n\n\nThrough midweek...\n\nAn upper-level ridge across the central portion of the country and\ndownstream low pressure/troughing over the East Coast associated\nwith a departing major Nor'Easter event keeps has our forecast \narea beneath northwest flow aloft. This should keep the area dry \nheaded into midweek and allow for daytime highs to nudge over the \n90 degree mark Tuesday through Thursday. By Thursday and\nespecially into the weekend, humidity will be noticeably on the\nincrease as a low pressure system in the Southwest US interacts\nwith the remnants of current East Pacific Hurricane Polo edges\ncloser to our area. This low will reinforce a seasonably robust\ncold frontal boundary to our Northwest and to the south and east \nof this developing activity, winds will begin to shift southerly, \ntransporting, rich, moist Gulf air northward. Dewpoints begin the \nweak in the 50s F but likely rise to near 70 F into the weekend. \nIn the meantime, full sun in the afternoons will support mixing \nand drying with drought conditions still present. For this reason,\nthe \"Limited\" fire weather risk graphic was maintained for \nnorthern portions of the area through early this week. \n\n\nLate week into the weekend....\n\nWhile guidance has trended wetter overall over the next 7-10 days,\nmostly due to the GFS coming around to more of the signal shown\nin the Euro guidance, modeling continues to struggle both with the\ncomplex interaction of the baroclinic low and any tropical\nresiduals but also with the intensity of the subtropical ridge\nthat has been our hot, fair weather companion since Spring\nabandoned us. This results in higher than typical uncertainty in\nexact frontal progress through the weekend and perhaps even into\nnext week. That said, there is growing confidence that increased  \nPWAT values (likely at some point in the 5-8 day range exceeding \n2 inches) coupled with some improvement in deep layer flow amid \nincreasing upper southwesterly influence will likely result in at \nleast some risk of widespread, potentially at times heavy, \nrainfall. It remains too early to give more than very broad \nestimates of rainfall totals for the area, but current 50th \npercentile estimates suggest an inch or two could be common. \nShould some of the more aggressive members of the model ensemble \nsuite prove more accurate, some risk for flooding in some places \ncan't be ruled out. For now, while thunder would be likely at \ntimes, the system seems unlikely to pair adequate instability with\nsufficiently high deep layer shear to a degree necessary to pose \na substantial threat for organized severe weather. \n\nWe certainly could use a healthy rain as most of the area boasts \na 2 to 4 inch rainfall deficit for the last 30 days with dry \nconditions preceding. October is often the driest month for our \narea and this event may present one of the better opportunities \nfor widespread rain. Trends for hazardous weather possibilities associated\nwith this activity will be monitored over the coming days. /86/NF/",
